1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,395
Auckland has fifty-four volcanoes. I
watched the newest one being born.

2
00:00:05,765 --> 00:00:08,885
It took four days. This is
how it happened.

3
00:00:09,755 --> 00:00:13,910
Lui, Goldie and Spot get a pinch
each, not the whole tin.

4
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:19,070
Mum studied volcanoes. Dad
drove the number 72 bus.

5
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:23,735
Lui had two goldfish in the
pond. And I had a notebook.

6
00:00:24,105 --> 00:00:25,595
I was twelve.

7
00:00:26,105 --> 00:00:29,970
Every Monday, my class tested the
creek behind our school.

8
00:00:30,340 --> 00:00:32,955
Fourteen degrees. Same as always.

9
00:00:33,325 --> 00:00:35,865
Fourteen again? You and
your nerd notebook.

10
00:00:36,235 --> 00:00:38,070
Nerds notice things.

11
00:00:38,740 --> 00:00:41,205
Everyone, step away from the trees!

12
00:00:41,575 --> 00:00:45,515
By lunchtime, every phone in the
city showed the same alert.

13
00:00:45,885 --> 00:00:52,065
Ten kilometres under the city, hot melted
rock was rising, cracking its way up.

14
00:00:52,435 --> 00:00:55,550
Auckland is built on
fifty-three volcanoes.

15
00:00:55,920 --> 00:00:59,985
Almost all of them erupted just
once, each in a new place.

16
00:01:00,355 --> 00:01:03,570
So nobody could say where the
next one would come up.

17
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:06,355
Mum didn't come home for dinner.

18
00:01:06,725 --> 00:01:09,540
She was at the monitoring
centre all night.

19
00:01:09,910 --> 00:01:14,980
It's about ten kilometres down, and it's
rising. It could take a few days.

20
00:01:15,550 --> 00:01:17,135
Where will it come out?

21
00:01:17,705 --> 00:01:19,720
We don't know yet.

22
00:01:20,090 --> 00:01:22,605
That's what we're trying to work out.

23
00:01:22,975 --> 00:01:24,790
A city is noisy.

24
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:30,486
Trucks and trains blur tiny earthquakes, so
the scientists could only roughly tell

25
00:01:30,486 --> 00:01:31,525
where they were.

26
00:01:31,895 --> 00:01:34,680
Is the volcano going to
come up under my bed?

27
00:01:35,050 --> 00:01:35,860
No.

28
00:01:37,050 --> 00:01:39,810
The city is huge, and your bed is tiny.

29
00:01:40,180 --> 00:01:41,895
Drop, cover, hold!

30
00:01:42,265 --> 00:01:44,205
Under the table, both of you!

31
00:01:44,575 --> 00:01:48,720
Just in case. Water, torch,
radio, and the first-aid kit.

32
00:01:49,090 --> 00:01:51,255
It lives by the door now.

33
00:01:51,625 --> 00:01:53,790
It's fizzing. Like lemonade.

34
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:58,555
Nineteen degrees. Overnight, the
creek had warmed up by five.

35
00:01:58,925 --> 00:02:02,665
Nearly every volcano in
Auckland is a one-off.

36
00:02:03,035 --> 00:02:06,100
It erupts once, and then never again.

37
00:02:06,470 --> 00:02:12,275
So the next one would open somewhere brand
new: under a road, a field, or a house.

38
00:02:12,645 --> 00:02:17,435
NEWS ANCHOR: Scientists say their best guess is
under the harbour, but they can't be sure yet.

39
00:02:17,805 --> 00:02:20,120
Ana! Goldie and Spot aren't moving!

40
00:02:20,490 --> 00:02:23,605
Oh, Lui. I'm sorry. They
were fine this morning.

41
00:02:23,975 --> 00:02:26,860
And the pond's right at the
bottom of the garden.

42
00:02:27,230 --> 00:02:31,860
On his last run, the back of Dad's bus
scraped on a bump in the road.

43
00:02:32,230 --> 00:02:34,395
It hadn't been there last week.

44
00:02:34,765 --> 00:02:37,280
There's a new bump on Kōwhai Street.

45
00:02:37,650 --> 00:02:39,890
My bus scraped right over it.

46
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:43,050
Kōwhai Street? That's right
next to the creek.

47
00:02:43,420 --> 00:02:45,460
I put all three on a map.

48
00:02:45,830 --> 00:02:48,795
They were all within a
kilometre of our house.

49
00:02:49,165 --> 00:02:53,330
Before magma reaches the surface,
gas leaks out ahead of it.

50
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:58,340
That gas is heavier than air, so it
sinks into hollows and ponds.

51
00:02:58,710 --> 00:03:03,065
And as the magma pushes upward,
the ground above it rises.

52
00:03:03,435 --> 00:03:07,050
Dad, can you drive me to the creek?
I want to check it again.

53
00:03:07,420 --> 00:03:09,715
Get your coat. I'll get the torch.

54
00:03:10,385 --> 00:03:13,375
Twenty-one degrees. It's
still getting warmer.

55
00:03:13,745 --> 00:03:17,135
Dad, feel this. Warm air is
coming out of the crack.

56
00:03:17,505 --> 00:03:19,720
Call your mother. Now.

57
00:03:20,390 --> 00:03:23,225
Mum, I think I know where
it's going to come up.

58
00:03:23,595 --> 00:03:26,285
Ana, love, I'm really busy.

59
00:03:26,655 --> 00:03:29,395
Can it wait until tomorrow?

60
00:03:29,765 --> 00:03:31,050
No, Mum.

61
00:03:31,765 --> 00:03:35,000
Our creek is fizzing, and it's
five degrees warmer.

62
00:03:35,370 --> 00:03:40,455
Lui's fish died in the hollow, and the
road on Kōwhai Street is rising.

63
00:03:40,825 --> 00:03:43,640
Say that again. Slowly.

64
00:03:44,610 --> 00:03:48,795
The nearest GPS station has risen four
centimetres since this morning.

65
00:03:49,165 --> 00:03:54,235
We have a handful of instruments for a whole
city. She just gave us three more.

66
00:03:54,905 --> 00:03:57,830
Send the gas team to Kōwhai Street.

67
00:03:58,200 --> 00:03:59,365
Right now.

68
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:05,480
Just before midnight, the gas team's meters
started beeping on Kōwhai Street.

69
00:04:05,850 --> 00:04:08,520
Ana? The gas team found it.

70
00:04:08,890 --> 00:04:12,180
You were right. Pack a bag, love.

71
00:04:12,950 --> 00:04:16,490
On Wednesday morning, the
evacuation zone moved.

72
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:19,000
Our street was right in the middle.

73
00:04:19,370 --> 00:04:21,935
Every phone in the zone told us to leave.

74
00:04:22,305 --> 00:04:25,370
Twenty minutes. Take the
things you can't replace.

75
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:29,445
I took a photo of the four of
us, and my notebook.

76
00:04:29,815 --> 00:04:31,950
Mrs. Kaur, don't go down there!

77
00:04:32,320 --> 00:04:34,655
The gas sinks into low places.

78
00:04:35,025 --> 00:04:38,340
I shook a box of cat biscuits
at the hatch instead.

79
00:04:38,710 --> 00:04:41,100
Cats always come for biscuits.

80
00:04:41,470 --> 00:04:44,035
Dad lowered the ramp for Mr. Tane.

81
00:04:44,405 --> 00:04:47,145
Nobody on our street was left behind.

82
00:04:47,515 --> 00:04:49,555
On you get, Mrs. Kaur.

83
00:04:49,925 --> 00:04:52,040
Your cat rides free today.

84
00:04:52,410 --> 00:04:55,540
I looked back at our house
from the bus door.

85
00:04:56,510 --> 00:05:00,215
Every lane of the motorway
ran one way: out.

86
00:05:00,585 --> 00:05:04,665
Forty thousand people left their
homes in a day and a half.

87
00:05:05,035 --> 00:05:09,900
NEWS ANCHOR: Scientists say a tip from a member of
the public helped them find the right place.

88
00:05:10,270 --> 00:05:12,760
That's you! You're a member of the public!

89
00:05:13,130 --> 00:05:14,815
Will our house be okay?

90
00:05:15,185 --> 00:05:17,650
I don't know, Lui. But we will be.

91
00:05:18,420 --> 00:05:20,835
I'm going back for another load.

92
00:05:21,205 --> 00:05:23,735
Look after your brother for me.

93
00:05:24,105 --> 00:05:25,865
Mum, what if I'm wrong?

94
00:05:26,235 --> 00:05:29,585
Forty thousand people left
because of my map.

95
00:05:29,955 --> 00:05:33,510
Then they go home on Friday, a bit grumpy.

96
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:37,045
Being wrong that way is fine, love.

97
00:05:37,415 --> 00:05:42,005
On Thursday afternoon, a police drone
flew over our empty street.

98
00:05:42,375 --> 00:05:44,790
The duck pond in the park was steaming.

99
00:05:45,160 --> 00:05:47,895
It's less than a kilometre down now.

100
00:05:48,265 --> 00:05:50,625
Keep everyone out of the zone.

101
00:05:50,995 --> 00:05:54,785
At the hall, I wrote down the
depth from the TV every hour.

102
00:05:55,155 --> 00:05:57,545
The numbers kept getting smaller.

103
00:05:57,915 --> 00:06:01,155
NEWS ANCHOR: The zone is empty,
and the power there is off.

104
00:06:01,525 --> 00:06:03,840
Do not go back for any reason.

105
00:06:04,210 --> 00:06:06,675
Everyone in the zone is out.

106
00:06:07,610 --> 00:06:11,200
Your map did that. I'm so proud of you.

107
00:06:11,970 --> 00:06:13,680
Lui found the fish.

108
00:06:14,050 --> 00:06:15,935
And Dad found the bump.

109
00:06:16,305 --> 00:06:20,910
Six hundred years ago, people here
watched Rangitoto erupt.

110
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:24,645
Their footprints are still
there, in the hardened ash.

111
00:06:25,015 --> 00:06:29,885
Grown-ups, children, and dogs. They
didn't run. They walked away.

112
00:06:30,255 --> 00:06:32,995
Rangitoto rose straight out of the sea.

113
00:06:33,365 --> 00:06:37,955
Today it's covered in forest, and
families walk up it on Sundays.

114
00:06:38,325 --> 00:06:40,555
That night, I couldn't sleep.

115
00:06:41,725 --> 00:06:45,465
At eight minutes to
midnight, the ground opened.

116
00:06:46,235 --> 00:06:50,240
I counted the seconds, the way Dad
taught us to with lightning.

117
00:06:50,610 --> 00:06:52,175
One, two, three…

118
00:06:53,145 --> 00:06:56,010
At twenty-three, the boom hit the windows.

119
00:06:56,380 --> 00:07:00,445
Sound takes twenty-three seconds
to travel eight kilometres.

120
00:07:00,815 --> 00:07:04,305
A volunteer read out every
name from our street.

121
00:07:04,675 --> 00:07:07,190
Every single person answered.

122
00:07:07,560 --> 00:07:13,474
Where magma meets groundwater, it explodes
into steam, and a cloud of ash and rock

123
00:07:13,474 --> 00:07:15,565
rushes out across the ground.

124
00:07:15,935 --> 00:07:18,590
Everyone it reached had already left.

125
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:21,045
Is that the park near our house?

126
00:07:21,415 --> 00:07:22,430
Yes.

127
00:07:25,015 --> 00:07:27,780
It's right at the end of our street.

128
00:07:28,950 --> 00:07:33,465
By morning, ash covered half the
city, and the airport was closed.

129
00:07:33,835 --> 00:07:36,650
NEWS ANCHOR: About two hundred
homes were destroyed.

130
00:07:37,020 --> 00:07:39,860
Nobody inside the zone was hurt.

131
00:07:40,230 --> 00:07:42,410
Ours was one of them.

132
00:07:43,580 --> 00:07:46,200
We lost a house. Look around you.

133
00:07:46,570 --> 00:07:48,560
We didn't lose anyone.

134
00:07:48,930 --> 00:07:53,645
For two months, it fountained lava every
night and built itself a hill.

135
00:07:54,015 --> 00:07:55,905
Then it stopped, for good.

136
00:07:56,275 --> 00:07:59,215
When it was safe, Mum took
me to the fence.

137
00:07:59,585 --> 00:08:05,355
Our street was under three metres of ash. I
could see the top of our letterbox.

138
00:08:05,725 --> 00:08:08,965
We moved into a house on the
other side of town.

139
00:08:09,335 --> 00:08:11,450
I hung our photo up first.

140
00:08:11,820 --> 00:08:15,790
Lui got two new goldfish. He
named them Gas and Bump.

141
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:22,037
Now schools all over the city test their
creeks every week, and send the numbers to

142
00:08:22,037 --> 00:08:23,665
the volcano scientists.

143
00:08:24,035 --> 00:08:28,540
A year later, the new hill had grass
on it, and a path to the top.

144
00:08:28,910 --> 00:08:32,815
Mana whenua, the people of this
land, gave it its name.

145
00:08:33,185 --> 00:08:36,050
Auckland has fifty-four volcanoes now.

146
00:08:36,420 --> 00:08:39,535
The newest one is where my
bedroom used to be.

147
00:08:40,905 --> 00:08:45,175
I study volcanoes now, like Mum.
I still keep a notebook.

148
00:08:46,345 --> 00:08:51,115
If you had twenty minutes to leave, what
would you take? Tell us in the comments.
